  "scriptId": "am-06-draw-off-replace-once",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Draw-Off and Replace: One Operation",
    "description": "Track what leaves a well-mixed vessel, then update the components after one replacement.",
    "teachingApproach": "Establish that every removed sample has the same composition as the vessel at that moment. Practise proportional removal first, then add pure water or the second liquid, solve target-ratio equations, and work backwards from final ratios to unknown original amounts. Keep all component bookkeeping visible so the learner understands the shortcut rather than memorising it.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 29,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 21, Alligation or Mixture. Approved scope: Teach the key idea that a well-mixed sample has the same composition as the whole vessel. When x litres are drawn off, each component leaves in its current fraction; only after that is the replacement added. Cover one draw-and-replace operation with water or the second liquid, solving for the withdrawal required to reach a target ratio, and reverse questions where the final ratio and the withdrawal reveal the original quantity. Include the chapter patterns 7 : 5 with a fixed amount removed and replaced by B, and 3 : 5 water-syrup changed to equal parts by replacement with water. Use direct component bookkeeping before any shortcut so learners can see why the algebra works."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn what really leaves when a mixed liquid is removed.\n\nIf milk and water are well mixed, a 5-litre sample has the same milk-water ratio as the whole vessel. First that mixed sample leaves; only then does the replacement enter.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A vessel has milk:water = 3:2. That means every 5 litres contains 3 L milk and 2 L water.\n\nIf the vessel is well mixed, any sample we remove has the same 3:2 composition.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "So removing 10 L removes:\nmilk = 10×3/5 = 6 L\nwater = 10×2/5 = 4 L.\n\nYou do not get to choose which component leaves. Both leave in the current ratio.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
