    "remedy_3": {
      "id": "remedy_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A common slip here is to mix up which number is a price, quantity, or fraction.\n\nInitial A=2, B=8. Remove x in ratio 1:4, then add x to A.\n\nFinal A=2−x/5+x=2+4x/5; B=8−4x/5. Set equal: 2+4x/5=8−4x/5 → 8x/5=6 → x=3.75 L.\n\nNow use the same idea on fresh values.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "retry_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "retry_remedy_3": {
      "id": "retry_remedy_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's rebuild this one from the quantities in the question.\n\nFinal milk=4−x/3+x=4+2x/3; water=8−2x/3. Equate: 4+2x/3=8−2x/3 → 4x/3=4 → x=3 L.\n\nTry it again with that relationship in view.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "retry_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A quicker form comes from the amount of one component that must change.\n\nIf water is the replacement, only the original component falls. After removing fraction f of the vessel, the original component is multiplied by (1−f).",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4_step": {
      "id": "teach_4_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For one operation, retained original component = original amount × (1−removed fraction).\n\nThis is the seed of the repeated-replacement formula.\n\nFor a single operation, direct component bookkeeping is usually easiest.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_4"
        }
      ]
    },
